// spec: a struct's member-wise construction beside its constructors (classes/struct_memberwise_construction_beside_constructors) takes a value for each of its bases and members, positionally (designated names may leave some out), each converting to its member; a class (the keyword) keeps C++'s rule, the error saying so
// error: struct_memberwise_construction_errors_is_error.kpp:21: error: no constructor of Loaded takes (int32, int32) (its members would take them in order, as a struct's do beside its constructors: a class's do not, design.md 30)
// error: struct_memberwise_construction_errors_is_error.kpp:22: error: no constructor of Pos takes (int32, int32, int32)
// error: struct_memberwise_construction_errors_is_error.kpp:23: error: no constructor of Pos takes (std::String, int32)
// error: struct_memberwise_construction_errors_is_error.kpp:24: error: no constructor of Pos takes (int32)
class Loaded
{
public:
  Loaded(const char* text) : x(text[0]) {}
  int x;
  int y;
};
struct Pos
{
  Pos(const char* text) : x(text[0]) {}
  int x;
  int y;
};
int main()
{
  Loaded a(1, 2);
  Pos c(1, 2, 3);
  Pos d(std::String("x"), 2);
  Pos e(1);
  (void)a, (void)c, (void)d, (void)e;
  return 0;
}
